# `lent`, the word for a C parameter that keeps nothing of what it is handed
# (panel 171, 2026-09-20), refused where nothing reads it.
#
# THE SHAPE RULE, and it is `check/marks.hero`'s own rule for the handle words
# applied to this one: a word nothing reads is refused rather than ignored,
# because a mark the compiler swallows is a claim the author believes is checked
# and is not. `lent` is read on a by-value `cstr` or `ptr` — the two types a lend
# produces — and nowhere else. An `@` position can hold no lend, and nothing is
# ever lent to a number or a handle.
#
# THIS IS THE WORD'S HALF OF THE FLIP AND NOT THE RULE'S. In this commit the
# parser, the printers and this shape check know the word; the rule that refuses
# a lend at an UNMARKED parameter lands in the next commit, with the marks on the
# compiler's own bindings that it forces. Two commits, because the seed compiler
# that builds the next one has to parse the marks that one writes — the one-commit
# landing was run and breaks at `selfhost/cli/process.hero:42:40` with no binary.
# So a `lent` on a `cstr` below is quiet here: read by the parser, unread by any
# rule yet, and deliberately not refused, or the next commit could not be built.
#
# CORRECTION, 2026-09-21, the next commit: the rule's half landed. A `lent` on
# a `cstr` or a counted `ptr` is now READ — a lend reaches only such a
# parameter, `lend_kept` refuses it anywhere else — so the two quiet shapes
# above are quiet because the word is true of them, not because nothing looked.
# And the sitting's completeness critic named a fourth dead shape the first
# commit accepted silently: a `ptr` that declares no extent, which no `ptr`
# lend can ever reach (`field_lend_uncounted` refuses it first), so the word
# there says nothing and is refused like the three below.

extern "lent.h"
    # The two shapes that carry it, quiet.
    function strlen(s: cstr lent) -> u64
    function read(fd: i32, buf: ptr counted_by n lent, n: u64) -> i64

    # A `ptr` with no extent: a lend never lands here, so the word is dead
    # text, and the message says which two words the parameter wants.
    #~v lent_shape
    function fill(buf: ptr lent, n: u64) -> i64

    # An `@` out-parameter: C writes into the program's own cell, and no lend
    # can stand in an `@` position, so there is nothing for the word to admit.
    #~v lent_shape
    function grab(@out: cstr lent) -> i32

    # A number is never lent.
    #~v lent_shape
    function bump(n: i64 lent) -> i64

    # A handle has its own three words, and this is not one of them.
    record Db tag sqlite3
    #~v lent_shape
    function close(db: Db lent) -> i32

function main()
    print(0)
